Brett:

Hard to be able to say much definitively about your study as your=20
attachments are snipped off by the list serve (a spam preventer measure).=

Also difficult to condone the use of tracks because not only of the=20
debatable representativeness of habitat along tracks relative to the=20
habitat of the study area, but also the potential for a density gradient =

of animals relative to the track (your grazing intensity comment is hard =

to use as justification for no gradient).

If your missing detection function appears as I suspect it might=20
(resembling Figures 2.2b and c of Buckland et al. (2001)), then you will =

likely need to change your field protocol.  As Buckland et al. (2001:32) =

note "if evasive movement occurs prior to detection, the estimator will=20
be biased low" and "It seems unlikely that methods will be developed for =

the reliable estimation of density for cases where a high proportion of=20
the objects moves in response to the observer prior to detection without =

making some very critical and untestable assumptions, unless ancillary=20
data can be gathered."

So you may need to investigate double observer methods that allow=20
g(0)<1, but you still need to fight your battle about representativeness =

of the sampled fraction of your study area.

